Your [[@lungs-breathe|lungs]] fill with air through your mouth and nose. Many sea cucumbers do something rather different. They pump seawater in through the anus into branching tubes called respiratory trees, take out the oxygen, then squirt the water back out.

That opening makes a handy front door for lodgers. Small, eel-like pearlfish live inside some sea cucumbers, sliding in tail first and coming out at night to feed. Tiny crabs and shrimps sometimes move in too.

{{story:earthworms}}

In the 1980s, Indian cities and villages had tens of millions of vultures. They cleaned up dead cattle within hours. Then, through the 1990s, they began dropping dead. Within about a decade, more than 95% of some species were gone.

Scientists finally traced the cause to diclofenac, a painkiller given to sick cows. When vultures ate a treated cow that had died, the medicine destroyed their kidneys. With the vultures gone, carcasses lay rotting for days. Stray dogs and rats multiplied to feed on them, which brought new health worries for the people living nearby. India banned diclofenac for animals in 2006. Today [[@vultures-india|vultures]] are being bred in centres in Haryana, Assam and elsewhere, and released slowly back into the wild.

{{story:dodo}}

Sound is just air being pushed in waves. Low notes push slowly, perhaps 50 times a second for a deep drum. High notes push thousands of times a second. Humans can hear from about 20 to 20,000 vibrations a second, though older people lose the highest ones.

The diaphragm in an earbud has to wiggle to match every one of those vibrations at once. It moves less than the width of a hair for quiet music. Bigger over-ear headphones have larger drivers, which helps with the deep bass notes, a bit like a big drum compared with a small one.

{{story:ears-hear}}

In 1714, the German-Dutch instrument maker Daniel Gabriel Fahrenheit built a reliable mercury thermometer. His scale put water's freezing point at 32 degrees and boiling at 212. In 1742, the Swedish astronomer Anders Celsius proposed a scale with 100 steps between freezing and boiling, but he put 0 at boiling. Others soon flipped it.

Today almost every country uses Celsius. The USA mainly uses Fahrenheit for weather and cooking, so a hot summer day there might be reported as 95 degrees. That is about 35°C, a very ordinary May afternoon in Delhi. The story of how the scales split is in [[@temperature-scales|temperature scales]].

{{story:temperature-scales}}

India is home to most of the world's Hindus, one of the largest Muslim populations of any country, and many Christians, Sikhs, Buddhists and Jains. Each faith has its own festivals.

[[@diwali-lights|Diwali]], the festival of lights, is celebrated by Hindus, Sikhs and Jains, each for their own reasons. [[@eid|Eid]] al-Fitr marks the end of Ramadan, the month of fasting for Muslims. Christmas is a public holiday across India. Guru Nanak Jayanti honours the founder of Sikhism. Buddha Purnima marks the Buddha's birth and enlightenment. Mahavir Jayanti honours the 24th Jain tirthankara. Parsis celebrate Navroz, their new year.

{{story:indian-calendars}}

Volume works the same way, but in three directions. A box 5 cm long, 4 cm wide and 3 cm high holds 5 × 4 × 3 = 60 little cubes, each 1 cm on every side. Its volume is 60 cm³, which is also 60 millilitres.

An Olympic swimming pool is 50 m long and 25 m wide, and at least 2 m deep. That gives at least 2,500 m³. Since each cubic metre holds 1,000 litres, the pool needs at least 2.5 million litres of water.

{{story:floating-ships}}

The scientist [[@vikram-sarabhai|Vikram Sarabhai]], founder of India's space programme, believed [[@satellites-help|satellites]] could bring education to remote villages faster than building cables and towers. He died in 1971, but his idea went ahead.

In 1975 and 1976, ISRO borrowed a NASA satellite, ATS-6, for a year. Programmes were beamed to about 2,400 villages in six states, in several languages. Villagers gathered around community TV sets to watch lessons on farming, health, family planning and science. It showed the world that a developing country could use space for daily life. Later, India launched its own INSAT satellites, which carried TV to the whole country.

{{story:spacecraft-move}}

Humans have a built-in reaction called the mammalian dive reflex. When your face touches cold water and you hold your breath, your heart slows down. Blood vessels in your arms and legs narrow, keeping more blood and oxygen for the heart and brain.

Seals, whales and dolphins have a far stronger version. The [[@blue-whale|blue whale]] can stay under for many minutes, while some beaked whales can dive for over two hours. In humans the reflex is gentler, but trained free divers, who dive on a single breath, can hold their breath for several minutes.

{{story:blue-whale}}

Antonie van Leeuwenhoek sold cloth in the Dutch city of Delft. Cloth merchants used magnifying glasses to check the quality of threads, and he became hooked on lenses. He taught himself to grind and polish tiny glass beads into lenses, some smaller than a pea, and mounted each between two metal plates.

His simple one-lens microscopes could magnify up to about 270 times, better than anyone else's at the time. Holding them close to his eye, he looked at pond water, rainwater, blood and the scrapings from his teeth. He saw swimming "animalcules", red blood cells and sperm cells. Over about 50 years he wrote nearly 200 letters to the Royal Society in London describing what he found. At first some members doubted him, until others confirmed his observations.

{{story:body-cells}}

[[@sound-travel|Sound travels]] as pressure waves. Picture a wave drawn as a wiggly line, with peaks and dips. If two identical waves line up peak to peak, they add up and get louder. If one is flipped, so its peaks line up with the other's dips, they cancel.

{{story:waves-how}}

You can see the same thing with [[@waves-how|water waves]]. When ripples from two sides meet, some spots rise higher and others go flat. [[@how-headphones|Noise-cancelling headphones]] try to make your ear one of those flat spots.

Just before the war begins, Arjuna looks across the field and sees teachers, cousins and uncles in the opposing army. He lays down his bow and refuses to fight. His charioteer, Krishna, whom Hindus regard as a form of the god Vishnu, answers him. Their conversation, 700 verses long, is the [[@bhagavad-gita|Bhagavad Gita]], "the song of the Lord".

The Gita talks about duty, action and the nature of the self. It has been translated into many languages and read by people of many faiths. Mahatma [[@gandhi|Gandhi]] read it often and wrote his own Gujarati [[@translation|translation]] with a commentary.

{{story:greek-myths}}

A good picture book text sounds simple, but it leaves gaps on purpose. If the words say "Max was cross", the picture can show what he did about it. Authors cut descriptions that the art can show better, and keep the rhythm and the jokes for reading aloud.

Many picture books are written to be read by an adult to a child. That is why the sound matters so much. Dr Seuss wrote *Green Eggs and Ham* using only 50 different words after a bet with his publisher, and [[@dr-seuss|his books]] still bounce when you read them aloud.

{{story:printing-press}}

In 2001 a group called the People's Union for Civil Liberties went to the [[@supreme-courts|Supreme Court]]. They argued that India had plenty of grain in its warehouses while children went hungry. The court agreed and ordered every state to serve cooked meals in government primary schools.

It was an unusual case: a court deciding what goes into a lunch plate. But it treated food as part of a child's [[@rights-kids|right]] to life and education. Over the next few years hot meals spread across the country.

{{story:rights-kids}}
